<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">New Guinea</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> is the world's second-largest island, north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>, with high mountain ranges and dense rainforest. Its western coasts were claimed by the Sultanate of Tidore. In the 1880s the island was divided between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> in the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in the north-east and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> in the south-east. The western half is now part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> and the eastern half forms <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/new-guinea.jpg" alt="New Guinea" width="320" height="194" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SaltedSturgeon · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Satellite_map_of_New_Guinea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>New <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, also known as Papua, has an area of 785,753 km2. It lies in Melanesia, in the southwestern Pacific, separated from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a> by the 150-kilometre-wide Torres Strait. Both landmasses lie on the same continental shelf and were joined in the Pleistocene, when sea levels were low, as the landmass of Sahul. The island has the third-largest remaining rainforest in the world and the highest plant biodiversity of any island. Its shape is often compared to that of a bird-of-paradise, giving the names Bird's Head Peninsula in the northwest and Bird's Tail Peninsula in the southeast. The Spanish explorer Íñigo Ortiz de Retes gave the island its name in 1545, because its peoples seemed to him to resemble those of Guinea in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Humans arrived on New Guinea perhaps 60,000 years ago, probably by sea from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a> during the last glacial period, although the date is debated. Their descendants developed one of the earliest known agricultures: the highlands were an early and independent centre of agriculture, with evidence of irrigation going back at least 10,000 years, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugarcane">sugarcane</a> was cultivated around 6000 BCE. Today almost a thousand tribal groups live on the island, with a near-equivalent number of languages, making it the most linguistically diverse area in the world.</p><p>The western part of the island had contact with kingdoms elsewhere in the Indonesian region; the Negarakertagama mentions Wanin and Sran as part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/majapahit-empire">Majapahit</a>'s tributary. The sultans of Tidore claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over various coastal areas, whose exports included resins, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spice-trade">spices</a>, slaves and the highly prized feathers of the bird-of-paradise. In the 1780s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuku-of-tidore">Sultan Nuku</a> of Tidore, who rebelled against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> colonisation, called himself <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> of Tidore and Papua. After Tidore became a Dutch tributary, much of its claimed territory came under Dutch rule as part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-indies">Dutch East Indies</a>.</p><p>Portuguese and Spanish sailors made the first known European contact in the 16th century. Jorge de Menezes saw the western tip in 1526-27 and named it ilhas dos Papuas, and in 1606 Luís Vaz de Torres explored the southern coast. In 1942 the Japanese invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> territory and the Australian territories in the eastern half. The highlands, northern and eastern parts became key battlefields, including those for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a>, Milne Bay and the Kokoda track. Papuans often gave vital help to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allies">Allies</a>, and about 216,000 Japanese, Australian and U.S. servicemen died in the New Guinea campaign.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The island is divided politically into roughly equal halves along a north-south line. The eastern half is the mainland of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papua-new-guinea">Papua New Guinea</a>, independent since 1975 and formerly administered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/australia">Australia</a>. The western half, formerly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, is divided into six Indonesian provinces. The two major cities on the island are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-moresby">Port Moresby</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jayapura">Jayapura</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “New Guinea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Japan entered the war, Singapore fell in February 1942 with the capture of most of the 8th Division, Darwin was bombed, and Australian troops halted the Japanese advance on the Kokoda Track in <b>New Guinea</b>, while Prime Minister John Curtin turned to the United States for protection.</blockquote><a href="/empire/commonwealth-of-australia">Commonwealth of Australia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>By about 1910 the whole archipelago, including much of <b>New Guinea</b>, was under Dutch rule.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-east-indies">Dutch East Indies · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Indonesia spans some 17,000 islands, from Sumatra to <b>New Guinea</b>, and its population has grown from about 70 million in 1950 to around 280 million, more than half of them on Java.</blockquote><a href="/empire/indonesia">Indonesia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>They came when Australia was part of Sahul, joined to <b>New Guinea</b> by land, but still had to cross the sea beyond the Wallace Line, perhaps island-hopping from Sulawesi or by way of Timor.</blockquote><a href="/culture/aboriginal-australians">Aboriginal Australians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Lapita pottery appears in the Bismarck Archipelago, north-east of <b>New Guinea</b>, between about 1500 and 1300 BCE; the exact date is still debated, and several recent studies favour the later end of that range.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Queen Tribhuwana (r. 1328–1350) and her son Hayam Wuruk (r. 1350–1389), with the prime minister Gajah Mada, the kingdom claimed tribute from 98 lands from Sumatra to <b>New Guinea</b>, though its direct rule did not extend beyond east Java and Bali.</blockquote><a href="/societies/majapahit">Social Hierarchy of Majapahit</a></li></ul></section>
